`jit earns` is `interp/row / jit/row` — what compiling bought for ONE activation,
with the call's fixed cost differenced out of both sides. It is the only ratio in
this file that compares two of OUR tiers on one unit, and so the only one that
says whether the compiler is doing its job independently of how the call is
entered.

`drv fix` is `jit fix - clean fix`. `Tier::Clean` dispatches straight to the plain
interpreter and takes no driver at all, so this difference is the pooled-driver
path's own per-call cost — the pool lookup, the program-table insert, the state
republish, the per-call state buffers — measured rather than itemised.

⚠ `interp fix` and `jit fix` both still CONTAIN one interpreted row: the batch
loop is bottom-tested, so row 0 runs before the first back edge on every tier.
That row's cost scales with the expression, which is why these intercepts differ
per case while `drv fix` is the part that does not.

⚠ `jit earns` is not a claim about a single call. It compares two per-ROW slopes,
and a call in cometkim's regime carries one row: whether that call is on the
`interp/row` side or the `jit/row` side is what `enter/call` reports per case, and
it is not the same answer for every case any more. A case whose `enter/call` is 0
pays `interp/row`; one that enters through the function-entry door does not.

