`resolve ns` is every declared slot path looked up in the batch's columns — one
map lookup per path, and nothing else. `encode ns` is what the rest of `bind`
does: build the buffers the caller did not supply (a `size(...)` length column, a
list's `offset(...)` prefix sums, a concatenation's characters) and rank the
batch's strings into ids. The library performs them in that order and `bind` is
the two composed, so a caller can hold a resolution and encode against it — which
is how `encode ns` is timed without re-resolving inside the timer.

`split err` is `(res+enc - bind) / bind`. It is the whole test that this is a
SPLIT and not a second measurement of the same thing: near zero, the two halves
account for `bind` and the row can be read; large, they do not and it cannot. The
answers are gated separately — the two-step bind must produce what the one-step
bind produces, checked per case before anything here is timed.

The three ratios are all `exec` over one of OUR costs, spelled as the division
performed, so ABOVE 1.00x we beat `Program::execute` and BELOW it that door beat
us. They answer three different questions and are not interchangeable:
* `exec/auto` charges our side for the EVALUATION only. A caller who binds once
and evaluates many times approaches it; a single-shot caller never does.
* `exec/a+bind` charges our side in full — evaluation plus the whole activation
encoding. This is what a caller who evaluates ONCE actually gets, and it is the
honest single-shot verdict.
* `exec/a+res` charges our side only for the work the other door also does: the
by-name lookup per variable that `cel_eval_loop` performs on every call. It is
NOT what a single-shot caller pays. It isolates how much of the single-shot
verdict is columnar SETUP rather than a difference between evaluators, which is
the one thing `exec/a+bind` cannot say on its own.

⚠ Read `exec/a+bind` and `exec/a+res` as a PAIR. Where they agree, the verdict
does not depend on how the encoding is charged. Where they straddle 1.00x, it
does, and the case is decided by setup rather than by evaluation — which is a
finding about the batch API's shape, not about either evaluator.

`clean ns` is the same lowered bytecode on the plain Rust VM, with no tracing
machinery at all. Where `majit` is far above it the cost is the tracer, not the
machine, and the compiled tier is not what answered the call.

`auto ns` is the DEFAULT door, `collect()`, which names no tier: the bound batch
picks between `clean` and `majit` by `save ns` — how much the compiled tier is
ESTIMATED to save on this run: rows times a per-row rate plus elements times a
per-element one, each rate set by that loop's word count less a fixed per-unit
cost a word count cannot see. Below `JIT_ENTRY_PS` the run does not save what
reaching compiled code costs, so it stays on `clean` — the batch machine's
untraced interpreter, not `cel::vm`. `words` is the same
run's size on either tier, printed beside it because the two were once one
decision. `route` says which side of that each case
landed, and `auto ns` should track whichever of the two columns to its left the
route named. `majit ns` beside it is still the compiled tier ASKED FOR outright,
which is what every tier-explicit test and every column of this table below
measures — the route changes the default, not the `_on` doors.

⚠ `stock/auto` measures our route against the TREE WALKER, `stock/majit` a
caller who names `Tier::Jit`, and `exec/auto` our route against
`Program::execute`. All three are spelled as the division actually performed, so
each cell checks against the two ns columns it comes from: ABOVE 1.00x we beat
the evaluator the numerator names, BELOW it that evaluator beat us.

The walker is NOT what a default caller gets, and `exec ns` is the column that
says so in numbers. `vm` is a default feature, so in this build `Program::execute`
reaches cel::vm::cel_eval_loop, the bytecode VM,
while the head of this file says why the walker is nonetheless the right baseline
for comparing against HIS published figures. So read the two families apart:
every `stock/..` ratio is against the walker and against nothing else, and
`exec/auto` is the one that answers whether we beat the evaluator a default
consumer actually runs.

⚠ `exec ns` is one whole `Program::execute` and the batch columns beside it are
not the same setup. `execute` builds its VM state — operand stack, locals, logic
slots — on every call and looks every variable up in the `Context` BY NAME; the
batch columns hoist both out, the activation having been resolved to slots and
encoded into columns at `bind` (timed on its own as `bind ns`) and the run state
belonging to the `BoundBatch` and reused call after call. `exec/auto` is
therefore a DOOR-to-door ratio — what a caller gains by moving to the batch API,
setup and all — and not two evaluators compared on equal footing.

Where the two disagree the route is
doing something: at one row a straight-line expression has a body of tens of
words, and no amount of compiling it pays back the entry.

`majit ns` holds the activation fixed exactly as his `execute(&ctx)` does, but a
majit activation was resolved to slots and encoded into columns at `bind`, while his
compiled code looks every variable up in the context BY NAME on every call. `bind ns`
is that encoding timed on its own — it is MORE than name resolution, so adding it back
is a pessimistic bound on the difference, not an estimate of it.

`enter/call` is calls that ENTERED compiled code, counted at the point the
compiled body is about to run — not artifacts minted. It now GATES the `majit ns`
cell beside it rather than merely standing next to it: a case that did not enter on
every call of the settled window prints `not entered` there and no `stock/majit`
ratio, because the number would be the tracing interpreter's under the compiled
tier's heading. A `0.00` with `compiles` at 1 is a loop that was compiled and never
run. Two ways in can produce a positive number: the row BODY contains a loop that
gets hot on its own, or the function-entry door — which counts CALLS, not rows —
warmed on the repeated one-row calls this file makes. The door declines for a
program whose own loop is already compiled, so which of the two applies is a
per-case fact.

⚠ The window this is measured over is the 1 000-call settled window, not the timed
loop: the timed loop cannot afford a counter read per call. A case that entered on
all 1 000 settled calls and then stopped would still publish a number. For the
per-call-evidenced form of the same measurement see `majit_percall_steady`.

`jit/row ns` is the compiled tier's MARGINAL cost of one more activation,
measured by replicating this case's single activation bit-for-bit into two batch
sizes and differencing them. Three counter gates stand in front of it and it
prints `-` if any fails: every call in a fixed 200-call probe entered compiled
code, every TIMED call entered it, and no compile or trace-abort happened inside
the timed windows. A number that merely looks fast does not get printed.

⚠ `jit/row ns` is NOT the unit cometkim's `compiled` column is in. His is one
whole `execute(&ctx)` including every per-call cost; this differences those away
on purpose. `jit fix ns` is published so they can be added back — but their SUM
models a call this engine cannot currently make at one activation, and quoting
`jit/row` against his number without it flatters this side.
⚠ A NEGATIVE `jit fix ns` means the two-point model is refuted for that row: cost
is superlinear in the replication count there, so extrapolating to zero rows
undershoots. Its `jit/row` is still a measured difference quotient between the
two sizes, but it is not a constant marginal cost, and neither cell should be
read as a per-activation figure.
⚠ Every replicated row is identical, so the compiled loop gets perfect branch
prediction and a hot cache. His regime has the same property — he re-evaluates
one fixed activation — so the comparison is symmetric, but neither side's number
is what varying data would cost.

The `record_*` cases are the only ones whose comprehension body carries a
CONTAINER. Every other body on this board is integer arithmetic over a scalar,
which is the one shape whose loop never puts a container on the operand stack —
and structured attributes are what CEL is actually used over.

⚠ `record_map_scaling/N` is a SIGN TEST against `map_list_scaling/N`, not a
paired control. Same element counts and the same `map`, but the two differ three
ways at once and the three do not share a sign.

In the BODY: `x * 2` is 3 instructions, 2 drops, 0 atomics; `i.price` is 2
instructions, 1 drop, 2 atomics — `LoadLocal i` clones the element's `Map` and
`GetField` pops it, reads through a reference and drops it again.

⚠ But a ladder-vs-ladder difference is NOT body-scoped, and the SCAFFOLDING
carries two more atomics that a body table cannot see. `IterAt` is
`value_index(sequence, index)`, which returns an OWNED `Value`, so it clones the
element every iteration; and `StoreLocal iter_var` drops what the slot held
before, which is the PREVIOUS element. Both are `Value::Int` on
`map_list_scaling` and `Value::Map` here. So the per-element difference between
the two ladders is: −1 instruction, −1 drop, and **+4 atomic RMWs**, not +2.
The clone and drop COUNTS are equal across the two; what differs is which arm of
`drop_glue::<Value>` each takes — the `Map` arm against the do-nothing one.

A difference of terms with mixed signs bounds nothing in either direction, so
what the pair answers is whether that container traffic outweighs the
instruction and the drop it saves, and nothing more quantitative. It cannot be
repaired by a better rung: a field read IS an instruction, so no record body
matches a scalar body's instruction count. The matched pair is
`record_exists_int/N` against `record_exists_str/N` below.

⚠ The column that answers it is `exec`, and ONLY `exec`. The three components
are instructions, drops and `Arc` traffic in `cel/src/vm/interp.rs`, and none of
them exist on `clean`/`majit`/`auto`, which read `items[].price` as a flattened
`i64` column with no `Value` and no refcount anywhere in it. Those columns
answer a different and also interesting question — whether the COLUMNAR tier
cares about record shape — and must be reported under that heading.

`record_exists_int/N` and `record_exists_str/N` ARE a matched pair, and the only
one here that earns the word: same `exists`, same instruction sequence, same
drop count, the same CONTAINER atomics, identical declared columns, and neither
predicate matchable so neither short-circuits. They differ in the field's type
and in nothing else.

Both are LADDERS over `map_list_scaling`'s element counts, and that is a second
question rather than a refinement of the first. `exists` answers a BOOL, so no
rung of either allocates a result list at any `n`, where `map_list_scaling/N`
and `record_map_scaling/N` each write `n` `Value`s and converge on each other
per element at n = 10 000.

⚠ But the pair that tells the two readings of that convergence apart is
`scalar_exists/N` against `record_exists_int/N`, NOT the int-vs-str pair. The
convergence is between a flat `Vec<i64>` and `n` separately-allocated
`Arc<HashMap>`s — an INPUT SHAPE contrast — and int-field against string-field
varies the field type at a roughly constant input shape. An instrument has to
vary the same thing the question does. `scalar_exists` binds the same
`Col::IntList` and the same `Value::list` of `Value::Int` that
`map_list_scaling` does, so `scalar_exists` vs `record_exists_int` is exactly
`map_list_scaling` vs `record_map_scaling` with the result list removed:

converges at n = 10 000 anyway  ⇒ the input alone reaches the ceiling
keeps separating                ⇒ the output list is what converged

⚠ What `exists` removes is FOUR things, not one. `run()`'s value is a temporary
dropped inside the timed span, so a `map` rung allocates a `Vec<Value>`, writes
`n` elements, makes `n` `drop_glue::<Value>` calls and frees it, EVERY timed
call. `exists` answers a bool and does none of the four. That is a much heavier
treatment than "no output list", which is why a null result here is informative
rather than merely disappointing.

⚠ And three of those four are the SAME work on both map ladders -- each produces
a `Vec<Value>` of `n` `Value::Int`, identical in type, size, allocation and free.
So no common-addend reading of the output survives: a shared cost added at the
top rung would raise both ladders by the same amount and leave their difference
intact, and the measured increments were 9.91 ns against 2.99. What survives is
INTERACTION -- the output evicting resident input, where the ladder with more to
lose loses more.

⚠ At n = 10 000 that allocation is ~240 KB, large enough that the allocator may
change regime (large bin or `mmap`) and take page faults a 24 KB one does not.
It is a PER-CALL cost, so segment slopes shed most of it -- but not all, since
the fault count scales with the size.

⚠ `clean`/`majit`/`auto` time `collect_into_on` into a buffer REUSED call after
call, so they amortize that allocation to zero, while `stock` and `exec`
allocate and free a fresh one every call. That is the deliberate fair door and
not a defect, but it means the VM columns carry one malloc+free per call the
batch columns do not, and at n = 10 000 it is a 240 KB one. It does NOT touch
the discriminator, which compares `exec` against `exec` and pays it identically
on both arms; it does affect `exec/auto` and `stock/auto` at large `n`.

⚠ The two arms build their activations differently, and this is harmless ONLY
because of WHERE the build sits. `run_case` calls `(case.stock)(&mut activation)`
ONCE per case, before any timer, and both VM columns then take it by reference
through `black_box(&activation)`. The scalar arm's build converts a `Vec<i64>`
into `n` `Value::Int` -- a 24N allocation, ZERO atomics. The record arm's clones
a `Vec<Value>` of `Value::Map` -- N ATOMIC INCREMENTS. Both scale with `n`;
only one touches a refcount, so the difference is in KIND and could not cancel
in a difference of the two arms. Anyone who later moves that build inside a
timed region has BROKEN this instrument, not perturbed it: the record arm would
then pay `n` `Arc` increments per call that the scalar arm does not, which
manufactures the "keeps separating" branch -- the exact result the pair exists
to test for.

⚠ The two outcomes are not equally strong. A SEPARATION is clean. A CONVERGENCE
is real evidence but leaves a residue: at n = 10 000 the scalar rung already
carries ~240 KB of input against a 128 KB L1D, so it is over L1 on the input
alone, and a convergence there is consistent with input saturation without
proving the ceiling is bandwidth rather than a level crossing. Read the weaker
branch as the weaker branch.

⚠ What that pair isolates is NOT the container's refcount traffic. That is FOUR
atomics per element and it is identical on both rungs: `IterAt` clones the
element out of the list, `StoreLocal` drops the previous one, `LoadLocal i`
clones it again and the field read drops that clone — whichever field is named.

What differs is FOUR MORE on the string rung, none of them on the container:
`value_field` returns `v.into_owned()`, so a string field CLONES its
`Arc<String>` where an int field copies; `LoadConst "zz"` clones the constant's
`Arc<String>` every iteration; and `Equals` pops both operands by value and
drops them. Int rung: none of the four. So the pair prices a string-valued
attribute against an int-valued one — a real question for policy evaluation —
and it does not price the loop.

⚠ The batch columns on the string rung are not comparable to the walker's.
`ValType::Str` carries a string as its order-preserving i64 RANK among the
batch's distinct strings, so the compiled tiers pay neither the container atomic
nor the answer's. `exec/auto` and `stock/auto` will therefore be wider there than
on the int rung for a reason that is about representation and not about the loop.

⚠ `record_filter` is NOT `filter_list_scaling`'s control. `i.price > 10` admits
989 of 1 000 elements where `x % 2 == 0` admits half, so the two run a different
number of appends per element. It is here because it is the shape a policy has,
not because it differences against anything.

task #88's decomposition, computed here rather than by hand. `majit fixed`
is the column the STOP-AT-P5 gate is stated in: its re-entry criterion is a
compiled artifact's fixed per-call cost under ~1 us. It is reported by TWO
estimators, in the second block, because one of them cannot disagree with
its own inputs and so cannot report an error against them.

